864f6ca39345415bb18c1cf775\",\n  \"status\": \"completed\",\n  \"task_tree_sha256\": \"sha256:3d535e54d20ac4fe18a1108503c7e7e5314ea546cf94653eccc611dc5caf1411\"\n}\n{\n  \"candidate_diagnostics_trust\": \"unverified\",\n  \"episode_count\": 5,\n  \"path\": \"work/results/a06/codex/20261105_100448/final_evaluation/results/evaluation_evidence.json\",\n  \"sha256\": \"sha256:4fac2b8c82f0ca0b7099446024b11ff1714ff18d3fb2875a1327f0c1fd750bae\",\n  \"status\": \"available\"\n}\n11:%             \\usepackage{iclr14submit_e,times}\n28:% Define iclrfinal, set to true if iclrfinalcopy is defined\n29:\\newif\\ificlrfinal\n30:\\iclrfinalfalse\n31:\\def\\iclrfinalcopy{\\iclrfinaltrue}\n32:\\font\\iclrtenhv  = phvb at 8pt\n48:\\textheight 9.0 true in       % Height of text (including footnotes & figures)\n49:\\textwidth 5.5 true in        % Width of text line.\n83:\\def\\@maketitle{\\vbox{\\hsize\\textwidth\n87:\\ificlrfinal\n196:\\newbox\\iclrrulerbox\n197:\\newcount\\iclrrulercount\n198:\\newdimen\\iclrruleroffset\n215:\\textheight=#5\\vbadness=10000\\vfuzz=120ex\\overfullrule=0pt%\n216:\\global\\setbox\\iclrrulerbox=\\vbox to \\textheight{%\n217:{\\parskip=0pt\\hfuzz=150em\\cv@boxheight=\\textheight\n218:\\cv@lineheight=#1\\global\\iclrrulercount=#2%\n221:\\loop\\setbox\\cv@tmpbox=\\hbox to0cm{{\\iclrtenhv\\hfil\\fillzeros[#4]\\iclrrulercount}}%\n223:\\advance\\cv@refno1\\global\\advance\\iclrrulercount#3\\relax\n229:\\def\\iclrruler#1{\\makevruler[12pt][#1][1][3][0.993\\textheight]\\usebox{\\iclrrulerbox}}\n231:\\ificlrfinal\\else\n232:\\iclrruleroffset=\\textheight\n233:\\advance\\iclrruleroffset by -3.7pt\n236:    \\put(\\LenToUnit{-35pt},\\LenToUnit{-\\iclrruleroffset}){%left ruler\n237:      \\iclrruler{\\iclrrulercount}}\n245:% \\colorbox[rgb]{0.7,0.7,0.7}{\\small \\parbox[b][\\textheight]{0.1cm}{}}}\n\"\"\"Candidate-owned prompt-bank and honest-report partition policy (CPU, stdlib).\n\nInterface: python_rpc_v1:handle. Every request/response is finite inline JSON.\nKeep this entrypoint self-contained or use installed packages: native smoke copies\nonly the entrypoint into its filesystem sandbox, not neighboring helper modules.\nNo filesystem exchange, model inference, labels, audit examples, outcome matrices,\nbaseline outputs, scoring feedback, or host paths are supplied to this process.\nArbitrary nonnegative integer seeds initialize randomness, never workload identity.\n\nOperations (additional request and response fields are forbidden):\n  reset: {\"op\":\"reset\", \"seed\": int in [0, 2**63-1]} -> {\"ok\": true}.\n    Initializes a fresh episode's RNG. Exactly one reset per independent episode.\n  update: {\"op\":\"update\", \"arms\": int in [1,16], \"confidence\":0.95,\n           \"slots\":[{\"key\":str, \"stratum\":str}, ...]}.\n    Exactly one update after reset. Slots are distinct unlabelled content hashes\n    (or synthetic slot hashes); length n is a multiple of four. Settings arrive\n    through these fields, not through seeds. The same API serves all workloads.\n    Returns exactly {\"prompts\":list[str], \"selection\":list[str],\n                     \"report\":list[str], \"weights\":list[number],\n                     \"tie_order\":list[int]}.\n    prompts: exactly 16 distinct instruction/format variants, each 1..4096 UTF-8\n    bytes, with DEFAULT_PROMPT at index 0. Bank must be identical for every reset,\n    seed, episode and workload; only its first `arms` prompts are executed. No\n    example-specific rules, answer keys, benchmark lookups, hashes, URLs or IDs.\n    Control characters except tab/newline are forbidden. Selection/report are\n    nonempty disjoint exhaustive lists of supplied keys, without repetitions.\n    Selection size is between n/4 and 3n/4 inclusive. Weights have report length,\n    and each equals 1/len(report) within absolute tolerance 1e-12. tie_order is a\n    permutation of range(arms), defining highest-score tie priority. No tensor or\n    binary values are transferred; there is no state-transfer operation.\n\nThe evaluator uses the actual returned partition and prompt texts. It selects the\nmaximum-accuracy prompt on selection rows, then reports its uniform mean and 95%\nWilson interval on report rows. It privately generates genuine model responses,\nowns targets, and compares against fresh audit accuracy (synthetic known truth).\nNo in-episode feedback/adaptivity is required. Invalid parsed model responses and\ncontext overflow count as incorrect, never disappear. Baselines: fixed, random,\nfull-sample winner/reuse, same-winner selector reuse, and honest half-split.\nMatched constant, copy-arm, contaminated copy-report, effective nonidentity input\npermutation and terminal reset/replay controls are evaluator-owned, not self-reports.\n\nScore: 1 - mean report MSE. Equal weights: synthetic/real families, n/K cases,\nseeds, four synthetic regimes and their replicates. Replay is a control, not an\nextra scored replicate. Missing episodes/controls retain MSE=1, accuracy=coverage=0,\nbias=optimism=width=reversal=failure=1. Search and final correct separately require\ncomplete valid experiments/controls and deterministic replay, NOT superiority,\nsignificance or a positive finding. Publication additionally requires a supported\npaper and reproducibility code; search eligibility does not certify these claims.\nMethods may change this policy, not the estimator or hidden evaluation objectives.\nAll complete JSON messages must stay below 8 MiB operationally and 64 MiB on wire.\n\"\"\"\nimport random\n\nDEFAULT_PROMPT = 'Answer the multiple-choice question. Return only the correct option letter A through J.'\n_random = None\n_updated = False\n\ndef prompt_bank():\n    variants = [\n        'Choose the best answer. Output only its capital letter.',\n        'Solve the question carefully, then give only the option letter.',\n        'Identify the correct option. Respond with a single capital letter.',\n        'Consider each option and select the best one. Print the letter only.',\n        'Your answer must consist solely of the correct choice letter.',\n        'Read the question and choices. Return the correct letter, no explanation.',\n        'Select one answer from the provided options. Give only its letter.',\n        'Please answer this multiple-choice question using one capital letter.',\n        'Determine which option is most accurate. Output the letter only.',\n        'Choose the single best option; omit all text except its capital letter.',\n        'Evaluate the alternatives. Give the correct answer as its option letter.',\n        'Respond to this question with the letter of the best available answer.',\n        'Find the right answer among the listed choices. Return only its letter.',\n        'Pick the most appropriate answer and output its capital letter alone.',\n        'Answer accurately. Use only the capital letter identifying your choice.',\n    ]\n    return [DEFAULT_PROMPT] + variants\n\ndef handle(request):\n    global _random, _updated\n    if type(request) is not dict:\n        raise ValueError('request must be an object')\n    if request.get('op') == 'reset':\n        if set(request) != {'op', 'seed'} or type(request['seed']) is not int or not 0 <= request['seed'] < 2**63:\n            raise ValueError('invalid reset')\n        _random = random.Random(request['seed'])\n        _updated = False\n        return {'ok': True}\n    if request.get('op') != 'update' or set(request) != {'op', 'arms', 'slots', 'confidence'} or _random is None or _updated:\n        raise ValueError('expected one update after reset')\n    arms = request['arms']\n    slots = request['slots']\n    if type(arms) is not int or not 1 <= arms <= 16 or request['confidence'] != .95 or type(slots) is not list or len(slots) < 4 or len(slots) % 4:\n        raise ValueError('invalid workload')\n    keys = [slot['key'] for slot in slots]\n    if len(set(keys)) != len(keys):\n        raise ValueError('duplicate slots')\n    _random.shuffle(keys)\n    midpoint = len(keys) // 2\n    report = keys[midpoint:]\n    _updated = True\n    return {'prompts': prompt_bank(), 'selection': keys[:midpoint], 'report': report,\n            'weights': [1 / len(report)] * len(report), 'tie_order': list(range(arms))}\nschema_version = 1\ncode_root = \"src\"\n\n[entrypoint]\npath = \"program.py\"\nlanguage = \"python\"\ntype = \"script\"\n\n[copy]\nexclude = [\n  \"**/__pycache__/**\",\n  \"**/*.pyc\",\n  \".venv/**\",\n  \"node_modules/**\",\n  \".git/**\",\n  \".mypy_cache/**\",\n  \".pytest_cache/**\",\n  \".ruff_cache/**\",\n  \".tox/**\",\n  \".nox/**\",\n  \"dist/**\",\n  \"build/**\",\n]\ninclude = [\n]\n\n[integration]\nkind = \"generic\"\nnotes = \"\"\n[\n  \"campaign_results_dir\",\n  \"count\",\n  \"generated_at\",\n  \"queries\",\n  \"reference_query_plan\",\n  \"references\",\n  \"results_dir\"\n]\ntask/task.md\ntask/task_contract.json\ntask/evaluate_final.py\ntask/evaluate.py\ntask/program_0/program_spec.toml\ntask/program_0/src/program.py\ntask/capture.py\ntask/requirements.txt\ntask/resources/search/synthetic_manifest.json\ntask/resources/search/split_manifest.json\ntask/resources/search/examples.json\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.bst\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.sty\ntask/resources/search/runtime_manifest.json\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/math_commands.tex\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/natbib.sty\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.tex\ntask/resources/search/paper_template/fancyhdr.sty\ntask/resources/final/synthetic_manifest.json\ntask/resources/final/split_manifest.json\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.bst\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.sty\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/math_commands.tex\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/natbib.sty\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/fancyhdr.sty\ntask/resources/final/paper_template/iclr2027_conference.tex\ntask/resources/final/model/tokenizer_config.json\ntask/resources/final/model/merges.txt\ntask/resources/final/model/model.safetensors\ntask/resources/final/model/config.json\ntask/resources/final/model/generation_config.json\ntask/resources/final/model/special_tokens_map.json\ntask/resources/final/model/tokenizer.json\ntask/resources/final/model/vocab.json\ntask/resources/final/smoke_examples.json\ntask/resources/final/examples.json\ntask/resources/final/runtime_manifest.json\ntask/resources/search/model/vocab.json\ntask/resources/search/model/tokenizer.json\ntask/resources/search/model/config.json\ntask/resources/search/model/generation_config.json\ntask/resources/search/model/special_tokens_map.json\ntask/resources/search/model/merges.txt\ntask/resources/search/model/model.safetensors\ntask/resources/search/model/tokenizer_config.json\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\ncodex\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
